;;; A conclusion's variable that no premise binds, in a rule only a
;;; commitment fires: two commands answered and two stopped — **W57**,
;;; regression `assert-var-under-commitment`
;;; (`corpus/regressions.toml`).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact `(p a)`, and a rule over `s`, which no fact holds, whose
;;; `:assert` writes `?y`, which its `:match` never binds. Root never fires
;;; it; a commitment holding a candidate `(s …)` does.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A conclusion's every variable is bound on every way its `:match` can hold
;;; (Q-M1e2.37), and a rule that writes one nothing binds is refused at load,
;;; whether or not any state fires it. Every command that loads the program
;;; exits 1 with the loader's line (`ein test`: 2).
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `2eb483e0`, release and debug alike, the commands disagreed, which the
;;; rule that no two commands may disagree forbids: `ein saturate` answered
;;; root's fixpoint and `ein solve --minimal` the minimal line, {∅}, each exit
;;; 0, never reaching a commitment; `ein solve` (describe) and `ein oracle`
;;; stopped at the first commitment that fires `r`, exit 1, with `unbound var
;;; ?y in :assert — bindings: {'x': 'a'}`. The engine now refuses it at load,
;;; on every command.
;;;

(relation p Node :why "{?1} is a node")
(relation s Node :why "{?1} is an s")
(relation q Node :why "{?1} is a q")
(p a)
(rule r ()
  :match (s ?x)
  :assert (q ?y)
  :why "{?x} is an s")
